  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and isolated thunderstorms expected today \nalong and east of US-29.\n\nThere has been an uptick in shower and thunderstorm activity in\nacross northern VA and central MD early this morning, especially\nin the last hour or so. This activity is moving steadily to the\nnortheast at 25 to 30 kts east of a slow moving cold front \nlocated east of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Radar estimates and \ngauge reports indicate hourly rain rates around half inch and as\nhigh as three quarters of an inch in spotty places. Additional\nshowers and possible thunderstorms are possible further\nsoutheast along increased convergence along the front in a very\nmoist air mass for early November. While the showers are moving\nsteadily to the northeast, the showers may exhibit slow \neastward progression due to mid-upper level flow parallel to the\nsurface front. By early afternoon, most of the shower/tsra \nactivity should be confined to southern Maryland and exit the \narea shortly after 21Z. Turning cooler overnight on \nstrengthening NW winds.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Turning cooler this weekend with dry weather \nexpected much of next week.\n\nBehind the departing cold front, expect a shift to a dry weather \npattern for the weekend and into much of next week. Northwesterly \ncold air advection will support falling temperatures on Saturday as \nhighs drop into the mid 50s to low 60s (40s to low 50s for the \nmountains). The trailing upper low passes overhead to the north \nwhich should keep some cloud cover around. Residual gradients also \nsupport a brisk wind at times with afternoon gusts around 20 to 30 \nmph, perhaps nearing 40 mph along the Allegheny Front. Winds remain \nsomewhat elevated into Saturday with a chilly night ahead. Forecast \nlows will be in the upper 30s to mid 40s, with spots of low/mid 30s \naround sheltered mountain and river valleys. Any frost generation \nshould be limited by the residual winds. High pressure tracks in \nfrom the middle of the country by Sunday. After a cloudy start along \nthe Alleghenies, expect abundant sunshine for the day with highs \nreturning to the mid 60s along I-81 and south of I-66 in Virginia. \nThis comes with west-northwesterly breezes around 15 to 25 mph. \nAnother chilly night lies ahead with widespread lows in the mid 30s \nto low 40s. Some patchy frost is possible where winds turn lighter.\n\nThe weather for the work week ahead appears mainly quiet. With the \nupper pattern being amplified, a series of troughs pass by to the \nnorth of the region. The initial height falls cross the northeastern \nU.S. late Sunday which drags a dry cold front through the area. An \nadditional dry system follows suit passing by on Tuesday. The peak \nin the warmth would be on Tuesday before temperatures fall again \nduring subsequent days. This would provide additional chances for \nfrost and/or freeze as overnight temperatures return to the 30s. By \nlate next week, an anomalous upper low settles overhead which may \nsupport a rather cold period heading into next weekend.",
